    summary: `The 15th Amendment promised that race, color, or previous enslavement could not be used to deny the vote. That promise changed American democracy, but it arrived with a wound: it protected Black men's voting rights while women, including Black women who had fought for abolition, were left outside the amendment's direct protection. It also did not enforce itself. Black men voted, held office, built schools, wrote laws, and reshaped Reconstruction governments. Then white supremacists used terror, fraud, poll taxes, literacy tests, grandfather clauses, and court decisions to drain the amendment of power. This page matters because voting rights history is not a straight line from amendment to access. It is a long fight over whether a right on paper can survive organized resistance.`,
